## 1. Introduction
Environmental hormones, also known as endocrine disrupting chemicals (EDCs), have been labeled as a class of chemical substances that can disturb the human nervous system and endocrine system, as well as weaken the immune and reproductive systems by mimicking the biological activity of natural hormones [1]. Among those EDCs with different structures and properties, bisphenol A (BPA) has attracted scientific and public attention due to its large production rates, wide usage, and significant estrogenic effects [2–4]. At present, BPA has been studied extensively for removal from aqueous solution, with the main approaches of physical methods [5], chemical methods [6], and biological methods [7]. Adsorption techniques, among the physical methods, have been used most commonly to remove BPA in aqueous solution because of simple equipment and easy operation. The adsorbents that have been studied and used to separate the BPA include zeolite [8], $\mathrm{Fe(III)}/\mathrm{Cr(III)}$ hydroxide [9], bentonite [10], minerals [11], and activated carbon [12]. Of those, activated carbon is used most widely, but due to the considerable limited adsorption capacity and short life cycles, activated carbon has considerable limitations as an adsorbent; therefore, it is of essential importance to develop new adsorbents for water treatment.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline materials which are self-assembled mainly by organic ligands containing oxygen or nitrogen with metal ions [13]. Compared with the conventional adsorbents, MOFs present fascinating merits due to their diverse compositions and structures, such as greater porosity, higher surface area and pore volume, tunable pore size, and modifiable surface [14,15]. MOFs have attracted much attention in the potential applications in catalysis [16], gas storage [17], separation [18], luminescence [19], chemical sensing [20], and magnetism [21]. Up to now, types of MOFs with various skeleton structures have been synthesized and explored mostly in gassorption-related fields. With the emerging environmental crisis around the world, research on the application of MOFs for the removal of the environmental contaminants has become more urgent and meaningful. During the past five years, the detection, adsorption, and degradation of heavy metals, dyes, pharmaceuticals, green house gases (GHGs), aromatic contaminants (nitrogen and sulfur compounds) in the liquid fuels have been reported [22–31]. However, study on aqueous system of pollutants on MOFs is limited. Several groups have contributed fundamental endeavors for the degradation and adsorption of some pollutants in the aqueous solutions [23–26,32–33]. In most cases, bulk MOFs materials display some shortcomings due to their micropores (less than $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ ), which prevent the access of bulky molecules to their internal surface [26], which obstructs the application of MOFs in the adsorption of macromolecules, especially in the aqueous phase. In order to overcome this obstacle, the supramolecule-templating method and linker expanding method are usually utilized to prepare mesostructured MOFs. As in the preparation of conventional mesoporous materials and in order to avoid the framework instability and interpenetration in a MOF with an extended ligand [34], the application of surfactant-templating in the synthesis of mesoporous MOFs is the best [26,35–36]. Moreover, the environmental friendliness and biocompatibility of the MOFs materials applied for the aqueous environment should also be well considered. Therefore, it is a still great challenge to develop suitable MOFs materials for efficient sorption and removal of aqueous pollutants.
The MIL series of MOFs, pioneered by Férey and coworkers, has exhibit attractive characteristics with large pores and outstanding surface areas [37]. However, some of MIL-MOFs contain toxic chromium, which may cause potential pollution issues. And the study of removal of BPA and other EDCs from aqueous solutions using MOFs has not been reported until now. Therefore, in our work, we focused on the hydrothermal synthesis of less-toxic MIL$53(\mathrm{Al})\{\mathrm{Al(OH)[O_{2}C-C_{6}H_{4}-C O_{2}]}\}$ and mesostructured MIL-53(Al). The sorption capacity and behavior of BPA on them in aqueous solutions were systematically studied in respect of sorption kinetics, isotherm, the effects of $\mathsf{p H}$ , and temperature to tentatively explore the mechanism of sorption. Remarkably, MIL-53(Al) in our study exhibited excellent sorption capacity of BPA from aqueous solution in comparison with other conventional porous sorbents. Moreover, the mesostructured MIL-53(Al) was demonstrated to present enhanced sorption capacity for the BPA removal when compared to the microporous MIL-53(Al), making MOFs promising candidates for the environmental applications in water pollution control and treatment.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Bisphenol A, N,N-dimethylformamide, and absolute ethyl alcohol were obtained from Sinapharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. Aluminum nitrate was purchased from Shanghai Sinpeuo Fine Chemical Co., Ltd., China. Terephthalic acid and Pluronic F127 $(M_{w}=12,600)$ were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich, USA.
## 2.2. Experimental methods
## 2.2.1. Preparation of MIL-53(Al) and mesostructured MIL-53(Al)
The sorbents used in this study were metal–organic framework MIL-53(Al) and mesostructured MIL-53(Al), which were synthesized by a hydrothermal method following the reported [38]. The detailed description of the synthetic conditions and compositions of the samples were reported in Supporting information. The products obtained were named MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 [(mesostructured MIL-53(Al)], respectively.
## 2.2.2. Characterization
X-ray diffraction data of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were recorded with a Bruker D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer using a secondary monochromator and $\mathtt{C u}\mathtt{K}\mathtt{\simeq}$ radiation (40 kV, 100 mA, $2\theta=5{-}70^{\circ}$ , and $\lambda=0.15406\mathrm{nm}$ ). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) photographs were obtained on an XL-30 scanning electron microscope. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption was measured on a Micrometrics ASAP-2020 sorption meter at $77\mathrm{K}.$ . Before analysis, the samples were degassed at $423\mathrm{K}$ under a vacuum for $400\mathrm{min}$ . Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method was employed to calculate the specific surface area in the range of relative pressure from 0.05 to 0.2, and Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) model was used to calculate the pore volume and pore size distributions of MIL-53 and MIL53(Al)-F127. IR spectra were recorded on a Nicolet 5700 FTIR spectrometer with samples in potassium bromide pellets at room temperature in the range of $500{-}4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The concentrations of BPA were determined by Shimadzu UV-1700 at a wavelength of $278\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.2.3. Sorption assay
In order to assess the time for BPA to reach equilibrium on MIL53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127, $1000\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ of BPA aqueous solutions was prepared with ethanol and water (the volume ratio of ethanol and water was 1:4). Then, $_{0.02\mathrm{g}}$ sorbents and $150\mathrm{mL}$ BPA aqueous solutions with concentrations of 50, 150, and $250\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (diluted from $1000\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ BPA aqueous solutions with water) were added into a series of $250\mathrm{mL}$ glass flasks. $150\mathrm{mL}$ water added $_{0.02\mathrm{g}}$ sorbents but without BPA was set as control experiment. Then, the flasks were sealed and shaken at $25^{\circ}C$ $\mathsf{p H}~6.5$ , and a speed of $200\mathrm{rpm}$ . The UV absorption degree of samples was measured by Shimadzu UV-1700, at a wavelength of $278\mathrm{nm}$ at various time intervals until the sorption equilibrium was reached. The concentrations of the BPA were determined by deducting the UV absorbance value of control experiment sample. The amount of BPA sorbed per unit mass of sorbent at time t $\cdot(q_{t},\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and at equilibrium $(q_{e},\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}).$ ) was calculated using the following two equations:
$$
q_{e}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})}{m}V
$$
$$
q_{t}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{t})}{m}V
$$
in which $C_{0}$ and $C_{e}$ $(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}}.$ ) are the initial and the equilibrium concentrations of BPA in the flasks, respectively, $C_{t}$ $(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}})$ is the concentration of BPA at time t, V (L) is the volume of the BPA solution, and $m\left({\mathfrak{g}}\right)$ is the mass of sorbent used.
Sorption isotherms were obtained by adding $_{0.02\mathrm{g}}$ sorbents and $150\mathrm{mL}$ BPA solutions of various concentrations (from $10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ to $400\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) into $250\mathrm{mL}$ glass flasks and shaking the flasks at $200\mathrm{rpm}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The influence of $\mathsf{p H}$ in the range of 2–12 on the sorption process was estimated, and the pH was adjusted with HCl or NaOH. The initial concentrations of BPA aqueous solutions were kept at $250\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ . The dependence of BPA sorption on temperature was studied at $10{-}50^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in shakers with $150\mathrm{mL}$ BPA aqueous solution of $250\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ , and $20\mathrm{mg}$ MIL-53(Al), or MIL-53(Al)-F127. The corresponding aqueous solution without adding BPA was set as control test in all experiments.
## 2.2.4. Regeneration of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127
In order to evaluate the regenerability of the sorbents, $_{0.02\mathrm{g}}$ MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 full of BPA were added into $25~\mathrm{mL}$ flasks with $10\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, respectively. The flasks were shaken at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ , and the sorbents were filtered and dried at $80^{\circ}C$ in an oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The regenerated sorbents were added into $150\mathrm{mL}$ BPA solutions with a concentration of $250\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ . The mixture solutions were shaken for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and then filtered by using $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ cellulose acetate membranes to measure the concentration of BPA. The sorption capacities of regenerated sorbents were calculated by Eq. (1). The sorption–desorption cycle was repeated 3 times.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of sorbents
The obtained MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were the target products and had uniform size and consistent shape (SI Figs. S1– S3). The surface area of MIL-53(Al)-F127 $\mathrm{^{'}}1008.3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) calculated from ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption data using BET equations was slightly greater than that of MIL-53(Al) $(931.3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{.}$ (SI Fig. S4 and Table S1).
## 3.2. Sorption kinetics
The sorption kinetics models that were used in this study to imitate the kinetic data of BPA sorption on the sorbents include the pseudo-first-order model and the pseudo-second-order model, which can be expressed as follows:
Pseudo-first-order model $:\ln(q_{e}-q_{t})=\ln q_{e}-k_{1}t$
$$
:\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
in which $k_{1}$ ( $1\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ and $k_{2}$ $:_{2}~(\mathbf{g}~(\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{min})^{-1}.$ are the pseudofirst-order and pseudo-second-order sorption rate constant, respectively. $q_{e}$ $(\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $q_{t}(\mathbf{mgg}^{-1}),$ are the amount of BPA sorbed per mass of sorbent at equilibrium and at time $t,$ respectively. The initial sorption rate h $(\mathrm{mg}(\mathbf{g}\mathrm{mg})^{-1})$ is defined according to Eq. (4) as:
$$
h=k_{2}*q_{e}
$$
The fitting plots and the kinetic parameters were determined by nonlinear curve fit, and the results were depicted in Figs. 2 and 3. Comparing the fitting results, it could be seen that sorption kinetic data fitted well with the pseudo-second-order model. The $R^{2}$ values (see Table S2) for this kinetic model were over 0.99 for MIL53(Al) and 0.999 for MIL-53(Al)-F127. The pseudo-second-order model represents that sorption rate is affected by two factors (e.g., the concentration of BPA and sorbent performance) to some extent, while the pseudo-first-order model represents that the sorption rate is affected by the concentration of BPA or sorbent performance. However, the two factors have significant effect on the sorption process. So, BPA adsorption behavior of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 followed pseudo-second-order kinetic model. Moreover, the theoretical value of the sorbed BPA amount on the sorbent at equilibrium, calculated using the pseudo-second-order model, was in good agreement with the experimental value at corresponding concentrations. The results demonstrated that the pseudo-second-order model was more suitable to describe the sorption kinetics of BPA on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127.
The sorption processes under isothermal condition basically can be divided into three sequential steps including external diffusion, boundary layer diffusion, and intra-particle diffusion. For BPA sorption of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127, BPA molecules in the aqueous solution diffused to the outside surface of sorbents and then passed through the boundary layer to the inside surface. Finally, BPA molecules were removed by interacting with the benzene ring and Al–OH on the sorbents after entering into the microporous framework self-assembled by terephthalic acid with Al ions via intra-particle diffusion. Generally speaking, the sorption rate in the initial phase was controlled by boundary layer diffusion, while intra-particle diffusion plays a decisive role close to the end of the sorption. The pseudo-first-order model and pseudo-secondorder equation cannot analyze the diffusion mechanism, so the intra-particle diffusion model was used, with the equation being the following:
$$
q_{t}=k_{i}*t^{1/2}+I
$$
$k_{i}(\mathrm{mg}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathrm{min}^{-1/2})$ is the intra-particle diffusion rate constant, and I $(\log{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1})$ is the coefficient associated with the thickness of boundary layer. If $I=0$ , intra-particle diffusion is the only rate-limiting step, otherwise, both intra-particle diffusion and boundary layer diffusion are the rate-limiting steps.
The fitting curve of intra-particle diffusion was shown in Fig. 4 and the fitting parameters in Table S3. Due to the nonuniformity of the sorption process, each sorption curve was fitted to two straight lines. From the figure and the values of I $(I\neq0)$ , it could be seen that the two lines do not pass through the origin. The result indicated that intra-particle diffusion was not the only rate-limiting step of sorption processes, and both boundary layer diffusion and intra-particle diffusion had an influence on it. However, the slope of the first lines was higher than that of second lines, and values of I fitted from first lines were smaller than that from second lines, which proved that the sorption rate in the initial phase was mainly controlled by boundary layer diffusion, and intra-particle diffusion was fast. While with the increase in the adsorption time, intra-particle diffusion plays a more and more important role in rate-limiting. When sorption equilibrium was reaching, particle intra-diffusion was the main factor affecting the sorption rate.
## 3.3. Sorption isotherm
The sorption capacities of BPA on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)- F127 were determined via equilibrium sorption experiments. From
Fig. 5, we can see that the maximum sorption capacity of BPA on MIL-53(Al) $(329.2\pm16.5\mathrm{\mg\y^{-1}}.$ was lower than that on MIL53(Al)-F127 (up to $472.7\pm23.6\mathrm{mg}9^{-1}.\nonumber$ , which possibly resulted from the bigger pore diameter and BET surface area of MIL53(Al)-F127. The mesopores diameter of MIL-53(Al)-F127 was $4\mathrm{nm}$ bigger than the kinetic diameter of BPA, which allowed BPA to enter the pores easily. The pore’s diameter of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 mesopores wall was smaller than the kinetic diameter of BPA, and the breathing of the MIL-53 structure allowed BPA to enter the pores [39]. Sorption analysis was conducted by employing the Langmuir model, the Freundlich model, and the Redlich–Peterson model which are described by the following equations. Langmuir model:
$$
q_{e}=\frac{b q_{m}C_{e}}{1+b C_{e}}
$$
Here, $q_{e}$ is the amount of BPA sorbed per mass of sorbent at equilibrium $\left(\mathrm{mg}{\bf g}^{-1}\right).$ ), $q_{m}$ is the Langmuir monolayer maximum sorption capacity $\left(\mathrm{mg}{\bf g}^{-1}\right.$ ), $C_{e}$ is the BPA concentration at equilibrium $(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}})$ , and $b$ is the Langmuir constant $\left(\mathrm{L}\:\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\right).$ . The sorption equilibrium constant of the Langmuir isotherm $R_{L}$ can be expressed by the following equation:
$$
R_{L}=\frac{1}{1+b C_{0}}
$$
Freundlich model $:q_{e}=K_{F}*C_{e}^{1/n}$
In this equation, $q_{e}$ is the amount of BPA adsorbed per mass of sorbent at equilibrium $(\mathrm{mg}{\bf g}^{-1})$ ; $C_{e}$ is the BPA concentration at equilibrium $(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}})$ ; $K_{F}(\mathbf{mgg^{-1}})$ ; and $1/n$ are the Freundlich constants that are related to the capacity and intensity of sorption, respectively.
$$
{\mathrm{:edlich-Peterson~model:}}q_{e}={\frac{K_{R}C_{e}}{1+\alpha C_{e}^{\beta}}}
$$
$K_{R}$ and $\alpha$ are the Redlich–Peterson constants; $\beta$ is a coefficient between 0 and 1. However, when $\beta=1$ , the Redlich–Peterson model and the Langmuir model are the same. When $\beta>1$ , the sorption on the sorbent is mainly single-molecule sorption and monolayer chemisorption-based.
The sorption isotherms and the fitting results of the Langmuir, Freundlich, and Redlich–Peterson models were shown in Fig. 5, and parameter values for BPA sorption isotherms were determined and listed in Table S4. The experimental data fitted the Redlich– Peterson sorption isotherm better compared to the other two models mainly because the Redlich–Peterson model is a combination of the Langmuir model and the Freundlich model and can describe non-uniform sorption. The $R^{2}$ values of the Redlich–Peterson sorption isotherm were 0.9869 and 0.9912 for MIL-53(Al) and MIL53(Al)-F127, respectively. The results suggested that BPA sorption behavior on both sorbents was not an ideal monolayer sorption. The sorption sites on the surface and pores of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were heterogeneous. There were strong and relatively weak interactions between BPA and sorption sites on the MOFs. The Redlich–Peterson coefficient $\beta$ was 1.4242 for MIL53(Al) and 1.8815 for MIL-53(Al)-F127, which implied that monolayer chemisorption of BPA on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 was predominant.
Compared to sorbents reported in the other literature (Table 1), the sorptive capacities of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were among the highest. When the BET surface area of the materials was taken into account, the high affinity of MIL-53(Al) and MIL53(Al)-F127 toward BPA was even more obvious. For example, the BET surface area of commercial activated carbons was 1084– $1225\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [40], which was bigger than that of MIL-53(Al) or MIL-53(Al)-F127. However, the BPA sorption capacity of MIL53(Al) was two to three times that of commercial activated carbons (ranging from 129.6 to $263.1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), and the BPA sorption capacity of MIL-53(Al)-F127 was three to four times that of commercial activated carbons. The high sorption capacity for BPA and the fast sorption indicated that MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were potential sorbents for water treatment.
## 3.4. Effect of $p H$ on sorption
The solution pH has an important effect on the BPA sorption process because it determines the forms of BPA in aqueous phases and the surface charge of the sorbent, thereby affecting sorption process and the sorption capacity. It could be seen from Fig. 6 that pH had an evident effect on BPA sorption capacity. The maximum sorption capacity of BPA on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 emerged at $\mathsf{p H}4$ and $\mathsf{p H}6$ , respectively. With the decline of pH, the sorption capacity decreased gradually as the $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}$ on sorbents in acidic medium was occupied by $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ , and the $\pi-\pi$ interaction gave adsorption affinity for BPA. At a neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ , hydrogen bonding between BPA and $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}$ on adsorbents and $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction were responsible for the sorption of BPA; however, with the increase in $\mathsf{p H}$ , the sorption capacity of BPA on the sorbent descended almost linearly, which may be consequent on that BPA was mostly ionized to mono- or divalent anions ( $\mathrm{BPA}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{B P A}^{2-}$ in basic solution and the hydrogen bonding between BPA and sorbents disappeared [8]. In fact, the adsorption capacity for MIL53(Al) at $\mathsf{p H}12$ was 0, which might result from the dissolution of MIL-53(Al) at $\mathsf{p H}\geqslant12$ which was confirmed by additional solubility experiments in alkaline solution. It could be concluded that MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were more stable under acidic conditions than under alkaline conditions, and MIL-53(Al)-F127 was more stable than MIL-53(Al).
## 3.5. Effect of temperature on sorption
Temperature is a significant parameter in the sorption process because it is related to the Gibbs free energy which declares the essence of sorption. The influence of temperature on BPA sorption was shown in Fig. 7. There was an optimum temperature for the sorption behavior of BPA on the two sorbents. The sorption capacity of the sorbents for BPA declined at temperatures lower or higher than the optimum temperature. It could be seen from the figure that the optimum temperature in the range of $10{-}50^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was $20^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The sorption capacity decreased with the increase in temperature as the increase in temperature enhanced the molecular thermal motion and weakened the interaction between the BPA and sorbent molecules. In addition, BPA tended to desorb from solid phase to solution phase when temperature was high due to the sorption progress being exothermic [43]. However, the thermal motion of BPA at $10^{\circ}\mathsf C$ or lower temperature was mild, which might reduce the probability of BPA diffusing to the pore surface of sorbent. So, the sorption capacity of the sorbents for BPA declined at temperature lower than the optimum temperature.
## 3.6. Sorption mechanism
We found that the MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 both showed good removal efficiency to BPA. The structure of MIL53(Al) is self-assembled by terephthalic acid with Al ions, and the mesopore walls of MIL-53(Al)-F127 are constructed from MIL-53(Al). It has been found that guest-induced structural transitions of MIL-53(Cr, Al, and Fe) family is the main reason for the striking gate opening and breathing phenomena [39] which provides entrance for the molecules bigger than the pores. Because the micropore diameter of MIL-53(Al) and mesopore walls of MIL-53(Al)-F127 are smaller than the kinetic diameter of BPA, the breathing of the MIL-53 structure was inferred to allow BPA to enter the micropores.
After sorbed in the micropores, BPA molecules would interact with functional group on the sorbents. However, the sorption sites on the surface and pores of MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were heterogeneous. The sorption in the micropores was explained by the breathing of the MIL-53 structure. $\pi{-}\pi$ Bonds and hydrogen bonding would be proposed for the sorption mechanism of BPA on the surface of MOF-53(Al) and MOF-53(Al)-F127 pores. The structure of BPA molecules (Scheme S1) included two benzene rings and phenolic groups. The contribution of $\pi-\pi$ bonds between the phenolics and the sorbents with benzene rings is undoubted [44]. Chen et al. [45] have reported that the $\pi{-}\pi$ complex was a very strong electron donor–acceptor system. In our sorbate and sorbents system, the phenol group of BPA was a charge donor, and MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 could be acceptor. The system was strong and was proposed to be the predominant sorption affinity of BPA on the surface of pores of MIL-53(Al) and MIL53(Al)-F127. Hydrogen bonding is another mechanism for the sorption of BPA on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127. In neutral and acidic conditions, the form of BPA is BPA molecular which contains two –OH groups. The interaction of $-{\mathsf{O H}}$ on the BPA with Al(OH) on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 might form hydrogen bonding (Fig. 8). Fig. 8 shows the sorption mechanism of BPA on MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127. In order to clearly understand the mechanism of BPA sorption on MIL-53(Al), more detailed works are necessary.
## 3.7. Regeneration of sorbents
The regeneration of sorbents can cut cost and dosage in removal of contaminants. In the study, methanol was used for the desorption of BPA as it has been used to desorb guest molecules from the pores of MIL-53(Al) [43]. The recycling and regeneration results shown in Table S5 suggested that the sorption capacity of MIL53(Al)-F127 to BPA declines approximately one third after three desorption cycles. The decrease in sorption capacity was mainly because there was still a small amount of BPA bound to the sorbents after methanol treatment. However, MIL-53(Al) still maintained a good adsorption performance after three cycles. The desorption after three cycles was $84.2\%$ .
## 4. Conclusion
In this work, we prepared metal organic framework MIL-53(Al) and mesostructured MIL-53(Al)-F127 which displayed satisfying removal efficiency to BPA. The sorption arrays of BPA showed that the optimum pH levels for the removal of BPA using MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)-F127 were 4 and 6 separately. The optimum temperature for the sorption behavior of BPA on the two sorbents was $20^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The pseudo-second-order nonlinear kinetic model was more suitable for description of sorption kinetics for BPA. The sorption equilibrium could be achieved roughly within $90\mathrm{min}$ for MIL53(Al) and $30\mathrm{min}$ for MIL-53(Al)-F127. According to sorption isotherm fitting value $R^{2}$ , Redlich–Peterson isotherm model could well describe BPA sorption by both MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Al)- F127, and the maximum sorption capacity was $329.2\pm16.5$ and $472.7\pm23.6\mathrm{\mg\y^{-1}}$ , respectively. Furthermore, this kind of material can be easily regenerated through methanol washing within $120\mathrm{min}$ . The sorption capacity of the adsorbents was well maintained $70\text{\textperthousand}$ even for three regeneration cycles. Breathing phenomena of MIL-53(Al) were speculated to the absorption mechanism of BPA. The interaction of the benzene ring on BPA with a hydrophobic benzene ring on the surface of sorbents and the hydrogen bonding of phenolic group on BPA with $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}$ on the surface of sorbents were proposed to sorption affinity between sorbents and BPA. We can concluded that MIL-53(Al) and MIL53(Al)-F127 have broad application prospects in the field of water treatment for their high sorption capacity and fast adsorption for BPA.
